var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"examples/power_nulls_compare/#Power-and-null-constraints","page":"Power and null constraints","text":"This example uses a linear array with explicit nulls and a piecewise sidelobe mask. The same specification is solved with LP, QP, and SOCP formulations to show how the formulation changes the optimization problem while the array, regions, and coefficient type stay fixed.\n\nusing ArraySynthesis\nusing ArraySynthesis: °, dB\nusing GLMakie\nusing Mosek, MosekTools\n\narray = uniform_linear_array(32, d = 0.5)\ncoef = ComplexWeights()\n\nThe sidelobe mask is split into angular intervals. This makes it possible to combine broad -20 dB regions with deeper -40 dB protected regions.\n\nsll_region1 = region(ClosedInterval(-90°, -69°), 1°)\nsll_region2 = region(ClosedInterval(-71°, -41°), 1°)\nsll_region3 = region(ClosedInterval(-39°, -30°), 1°)\nsll_region4 = region(ClosedInterval(-30°, -21°), 1°)\nsll_region5 = region(ClosedInterval(-19°, -10°), 1°)\nsll_region6 = region(ClosedInterval(-10°, -5°), 1°)\nsll_region7 = region(ClosedInterval(5°, 90°), 1°)\n\np = pattern(\n    beam(0°),\n    sidelobes(sll_region1, -20dB),\n    sidelobes(sll_region2, -20dB),\n    sidelobes(sll_region3, -20dB),\n    sidelobes(sll_region4, -40dB),\n    sidelobes(sll_region5, -40dB),\n    sidelobes(sll_region6, -20dB),\n    sidelobes(sll_region7, -20dB),\n    null(-70°, level = -55dB),\n    null(-40°, level = -55dB),\n    null(-20°, level = -55dB),\n)\n\nMinL1 gives an LP reference solution. MinPower adds an explicit quadratic objective; with SOCP, the same power objective is expressed through second-order cone constraints.\n\nresult_lp = synthesize(array, p, MinL1(1.2), coef, LP(), Mosek.Optimizer)\nresult_qp = synthesize(array, p, MinPower(), coef, QP(), Mosek.Optimizer)\nresult_socp = synthesize(array, p, MinPower(), coef, SOCP(), Mosek.Optimizer)\n\ntheta_vals = -π / 2:0.001:π / 2\naf_lp = 20 .* log10.(max.([abs(array_factor(array, coef, result_lp.weights, [ThetaDirection(θ)])[1]) for θ in theta_vals], 1e-12))\naf_qp = 20 .* log10.(max.([abs(array_factor(array, coef, result_qp.weights, [ThetaDirection(θ)])[1]) for θ in theta_vals], 1e-12))\naf_socp = 20 .* log10.(max.([abs(array_factor(array, coef, result_socp.weights, [ThetaDirection(θ)])[1]) for θ in theta_vals], 1e-12))\n\nfig = Figure(size = (760, 430))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"θ (deg)\", ylabel = \"|AF| (dB)\")\nlines!(ax, theta_vals ./ °, af_lp, linewidth = 2, label = \"LP min L1\", linestyle = :dash)\nlines!(ax, theta_vals ./ °, af_qp, linewidth = 2, label = \"QP min power\")\nlines!(ax, theta_vals ./ °, af_socp, linewidth = 2, label = \"SOCP min power\", linestyle = :dot)\nylims!(ax, -65, 2)\nxlims!(ax, -90, 90)\naxislegend(ax, position = :lb)\nfig\n\n(Image: Power and null constraints)\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Power and null constraints"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minl1/#MinL1","page":"MinL1","text":"Minimize a convex L1-type bound on the optimization variables.","title":"MinL1"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minl1/#Syntax","page":"MinL1","text":"MinL1(sum_limit)\nMinL1(; sum_limit = nothing)","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minl1/#Arguments","page":"MinL1","text":"Argument Description\nsum_limit Optional upper bound on the sum of the L1 auxiliary variables.","title":"Arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minl1/#Description","page":"MinL1","text":"Introduces one auxiliary variable per optimization variable and minimizes their sum. With LP and QP, these auxiliaries are imposed with linear inequalities. With SOCP, complex pairs can be bounded with a second-order cone.\n\nWhen sum_limit is provided, the model also imposes an upper bound on sum(t).\n\nMinL1 is a direct convex objective. It is not the sparse/thinned-array strategy. For active-element thinning through repeated reweighting, use IterativeReweightedL1.","title":"Description"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minl1/#Example","page":"MinL1","text":"using ArraySynthesis; using ArraySynthesis: °, dB; using HiGHS\n\narray = uniform_linear_array(32, d = 0.5)\np = pattern(beam(0°),\n            sidelobes(region(-90°..(-5°), 1°), -25dB),\n            sidelobes(region(5°..90°,     1°), -25dB))\n\nresult = synthesize(array, p, MinL1(), ComplexWeights(), LP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formulations/qp/#QP","page":"QP","text":"Quadratic programming formulation with polyhedral modulus constraint.","title":"QP"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formulations/qp/#Syntax","page":"QP","text":"QP()\nQP(polygon_faces = F)","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formulations/qp/#Arguments","page":"QP","text":"Argument Description\npolygon_faces Number of polygon faces for the modulus approximation (default 8).","title":"Arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formulations/qp/#Description","page":"QP","text":"Uses the same polyhedral (linear) modulus constraint as LP. The difference is the objective: QP allows a quadratic term, enabling objectives such as MinPower, MinIntegratedPower, and MinFieldError. The constraints themselves are always linear.\n\nRequired by IterativeFloorSynthesis.\n\nSuggested solvers: HiGHS.Optimizer, Mosek.Optimizer","title":"Description"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formulations/qp/#Example","page":"QP","text":"using ArraySynthesis; using ArraySynthesis: °, dB; using HiGHS\n\narray = uniform_linear_array(32, d = 0.5)\np = pattern(beam(0°),\n            sidelobes(region(-90°..(-5°), 1°), -20dB),\n            sidelobes(region(5°..90°,     1°), -20dB),\n            null(-40°, level = -55dB))\n\nresult = synthesize(array, p, MinPower(), ComplexWeights(), QP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/excitations/#Excitations","page":"Excitations","text":"The AbstractExcitation defines the parametrization of the array weights and, consequently, the structure of the array factor passed to the optimizer. Pass it as the fourth argument to synthesize.\n\nUse this page to choose the excitation parametrization. For exact constructor signatures, see the API Reference.","title":"Excitations"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/excitations/#Choosing-an-Excitation","page":"Excitations","text":"Excitation Array type Variables Best suited for\nComplexWeights ArrayGeometry Complex weights General unconstrained synthesis\nRealWeights ArrayGeometry Real weights Fixed-phase or amplitude-only feeding\nConjugateSymmetricWeights SymmetricArray Complex half-array weights Real array factor with maximum flexibility\nProgressivePhaseAmplitude Any array Real amplitudes with fixed phase gradient Steered beams\nQuantizedAmplitude Any array Discrete amplitude levels Quantized amplitude tapers (MILP)\nQuantizedPhase Any array Discrete phase levels Fixed-resolution phase shifters (MILP)\nQuantizedWeights ArrayGeometry Weights on a fixed constellation Discrete complex feeding networks (MILP)","title":"Choosing an Excitation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/excitations/#Real-Array-Factors","page":"Excitations","text":"ConjugateSymmetricWeights is the main option for centrosymmetric arrays. It enforces conjugate-symmetric weights and makes the array factor real by construction. On a SymmetricArray, ProgressivePhaseAmplitude also gives a real array factor while fixing the phase progression toward a steering direction.\n\nA real array factor is important because the modulus constraint becomes a pair of linear inequalities. See Symmetric Arrays and Real Array Factor for the derivation.","title":"Real Array Factors"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/excitations/#Excitation-Notes","page":"Excitations","text":"ComplexWeights is the general option: every element has an independent complex coefficient. Use it when no symmetry or phase model should be imposed.\n\nRealWeights keeps only real optimization variables. The array factor can still be complex because the steering matrix is complex.\n\nConjugateSymmetricWeights requires a SymmetricArray.\n\nProgressivePhaseAmplitude fixes a progressive phase reference and optimizes real amplitudes. If the reference direction is omitted, it is inferred from the first beam or shaped-beam region in the pattern.\n\nQuantizedAmplitude, QuantizedPhase and QuantizedWeights restrict excitations to a discrete set of levels, using Big-M constraints under the MILP formulation.","title":"Excitation Notes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/excitations/progressivephase/#ProgressivePhaseAmplitude","page":"ProgressivePhaseAmplitude","text":"Real amplitudes with a fixed progressive phase gradient toward a steering direction.","title":"ProgressivePhaseAmplitude"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/excitations/progressivephase/#Syntax","page":"ProgressivePhaseAmplitude","text":"ProgressivePhaseAmplitude()\nProgressivePhaseAmplitude(β)","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/excitations/progressivephase/#Arguments","page":"ProgressivePhaseAmplitude","text":"Argument Description\nβ Steering direction. Accepts a ThetaDirection, a UVDirection, a single angle (radians), a (u, v) tuple, or nothing. If omitted or nothing, the direction is extracted from the main beam in the Pattern.","title":"Arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/excitations/progressivephase/#Description","page":"ProgressivePhaseAmplitude","text":"Parametrizes the excitation as w_n = a_n e^jboldsymbolbetacdotmathbfp_n, where boldsymbolbeta is a fixed phase gradient and a_n in mathbbR are the optimization variables. The steering matrix is evaluated relative to boldsymbolbeta, so constraints at the look direction are exact and linear regardless of formulation.\n\nOn a SymmetricArray, the array factor is real (same as ConjugateSymmetricWeights). On a general ArrayGeometry, the array factor is complex.","title":"Description"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/excitations/progressivephase/#Comparison-with-ConjugateSymmetricWeights","page":"ProgressivePhaseAmplitude","text":" ConjugateSymmetricWeights ProgressivePhaseAmplitude\nVariables mathrmRe(w_n), mathrmIm(w_n) (complex, half array) a_n (real amplitudes)\nPhase Free per element pair Fixed by boldsymbolbeta\nReal AF Yes (on SymmetricArray) Yes (on SymmetricArray)\nBest for Maximum pattern flexibility Known look direction, amplitude-only control","title":"Comparison with ConjugateSymmetricWeights"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/excitations/progressivephase/#Example","page":"ProgressivePhaseAmplitude","text":"using ArraySynthesis; using ArraySynthesis: °, dB; using HiGHS\n\narray  = symmetric_linear_array(32, d = 0.5)\nbeam_r = region(12.5°..37.5°, 1°)\nsll_r  = join_regions(region(-90°..6.5°, 1°), region(43.5°..90°, 1°))\np = pattern(shaped_beam(beam_r, 1.0, ripple = -0.6dB))\n\n# Explicit steering direction\nresult = synthesize(array, p, MinSLL(sll_r), ProgressivePhaseAmplitude(deg2rad(25)), LP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)\n\n# Extract steering direction from pattern automatically\nresult = synthesize(array, p, MinSLL(sll_r), ProgressivePhaseAmplitude(), LP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minpower/#MinPower","page":"MinPower","text":"Minimize total excitation power subject to pattern constraints.","title":"MinPower"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minpower/#Syntax","page":"MinPower","text":"MinPower()","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minpower/#Description","page":"MinPower","text":"Minimizes sum_n w_n^2, the total excitation power, subject to the beam, sidelobe, and null constraints defined in the Pattern. The objective is quadratic; requires QP or SOCP.\n\nA common use case is minimum-power beamforming: fix the gain at a look direction (beam) and minimize power subject to sidelobe and null constraints.","title":"Description"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minpower/#Example","page":"MinPower","text":"using ArraySynthesis; using ArraySynthesis: °, dB; using HiGHS\n\narray = uniform_linear_array(32, d = 0.5)\np = pattern(beam(0°),\n            sidelobes(region(-90°..(-5°), 1°), -20dB),\n            sidelobes(region(5°..90°,     1°), -20dB),\n            null(-40°, level = -55dB))\n\nresult = synthesize(array, p, MinPower(), ComplexWeights(), QP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/iterativereweightedl1/#IterativeReweightedL1","page":"IterativeReweightedL1","text":"Sparse array synthesis via iterative reweighted L1 minimization.","title":"IterativeReweightedL1"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/iterativereweightedl1/#Syntax","page":"IterativeReweightedL1","text":"IterativeReweightedL1(; max_iter = 15, d0 = 1e-3, stable_iterations = 3)","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/iterativereweightedl1/#Arguments","page":"IterativeReweightedL1","text":"Argument Description\nmax_iter Maximum number of iterations (default 15).\nd0 Initial regularization to avoid division by zero when computing weights (default 1e-3).\nstable_iterations Stop early if the active element set does not change for this many consecutive iterations (default 3).","title":"Arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/iterativereweightedl1/#Description","page":"IterativeReweightedL1","text":"Promotes sparse arrays using iterative reweighted L1 minimization. At each iteration k, elements with small excitation amplitude w_n^(k) receive a higher penalty weight alpha_n^(k+1) propto 1  (w_n^(k) + d_0) in the next L1 minimization. This biases the solution toward fewer active elements without requiring integer variables.\n\nThe number of active elements is not set explicitly; it emerges from the pattern constraints and the array geometry. Compatible with LP and SOCP.","title":"Description"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/iterativereweightedl1/#References","page":"IterativeReweightedL1","text":"This package's single-pattern sparse strategy is closest to the sequential convex optimization approach in:\n\nB. Fuchs, \"Synthesis of Sparse Arrays With Focused or Shaped Beampattern via  Sequential Convex Optimizations,\" IEEE Transactions on Antennas and Propagation,   vol. 60, no. 7, pp. 3499-3503, Jul. 2012.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/iterativereweightedl1/#Example","page":"IterativeReweightedL1","text":"using ArraySynthesis; using ArraySynthesis: °, dB; using Clarabel\n\narray  = triangular_array(17, 17, d = 0.5)\nbeam_r = region(Circle(0.27, (0.0, 0.0)), step = 2°)\nsl_r = visible_region(Circle(0.27, (0.0, 0.0)); step = 4°, bandpass = 0.2)\np = pattern(shaped_beam(beam_r, 1.0, ripple = -1.5dB), sidelobes(sl_r, -25dB))\nobj = IterativeReweightedL1(max_iter = 15)\n\nresult = synthesize(array, p, obj, ComplexWeights(), SOCP(), Clarabel.Optimizer)\n\nCheck how many elements are active after synthesis:\n\nactive = abs.(result.weights) .> 1e-3\nprintln(\"Active: $(sum(active)) / $(length(active))\")","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/#Objectives","page":"Objectives","text":"The AbstractObjective determines what the solver minimizes or maximizes, subject to the constraints defined by Pattern. Pass it as the third argument to synthesize.\n\nUse this page to choose the objective. For exact constructor signatures and argument details, see the API Reference.","title":"Objectives"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/#Choosing-an-Objective","page":"Objectives","text":"Goal Objective Required formulation\nCheck feasibility only Feasible LP\nMinimize peak sidelobe level MinSLL LP\nMaximize array-factor response at a look direction MaxAF LP\nMinimize an L1-type excitation bound MinL1 LP\nMinimize a weighted L1-type excitation bound MinWeightedL1 LP\nSparse array synthesis IterativeReweightedL1 LP\nSparse synthesis for multiple patterns MultiPatternReweightedL1 LP\nMinimize excitation power MinPower QP\nMinimize array-factor power over a region MinIntegratedPower QP\nFit a complex array-factor reference MinFieldError QP\nIterative low-sidelobe synthesis IterativeFloorSynthesis QP","title":"Choosing an Objective"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/#Direct-Objectives","page":"Objectives","text":"Direct objectives solve one convex optimization problem.\n\nUse Feasible when the pattern constraints are the result and no secondary criterion is needed. Use MinSLL when sidelobe level is the quantity to optimize rather than a fixed constraint. Use MaxAF when the main concern is gain at one or more look directions.\n\nUse MinL1 and MinWeightedL1 when the direct objective is a convex bound on excitation variables. They do not implement thinning or active-element selection by themselves; sparse/thinned synthesis is handled by the iterative reweighted strategies.\n\nQuadratic objectives such as MinPower, MinIntegratedPower, and MinFieldError need at least QP.","title":"Direct Objectives"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/#Constraint-satisfaction","page":"Objectives","text":"Feasible only asks whether the requested pattern constraints can be satisfied. It is useful when another criterion is unnecessary or when debugging a new mask.","title":"Constraint satisfaction"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/#Gain-and-sidelobe-level","page":"Objectives","text":"MaxAF maximizes the real array-factor response in one or more look directions. MinSLL introduces auxiliary sidelobe-level variables over one or more regions and minimizes their sum.","title":"Gain and sidelobe level"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/#Norm-and-power-objectives","page":"Objectives","text":"MinL1 and MinWeightedL1 minimize sums of auxiliary variables that bound excitation magnitudes or components. MinPower minimizes excitation power, while MinIntegratedPower minimizes array-factor power over sampled directions.","title":"Norm and power objectives"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/#Array-factor-fitting","page":"Objectives","text":"MinFieldError fits a scalar, vector, or complex array-factor reference over a region.","title":"Array-factor fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/#Iterative-Objectives","page":"Objectives","text":"Iterative objectives solve a sequence of convex problems. They are useful when the desired behavior is not represented by a single convex objective, such as sparse element selection or peak-by-peak sidelobe correction.\n\nIterativeReweightedL1 and MultiPatternReweightedL1 promote sparse active element sets. IterativeFloorSynthesis follows a floor-constraint strategy for narrow-beam low-sidelobe synthesis.","title":"Iterative Objectives"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/feasible/#Feasible","page":"Feasible","text":"Find any feasible solution without optimizing any objective.","title":"Feasible"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/feasible/#Syntax","page":"Feasible","text":"Feasible()","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/feasible/#Description","page":"Feasible","text":"Sets the objective to zero and returns the first feasible point found by the solver. Useful to check whether the pattern constraints can be satisfied at all, or to obtain a baseline excitation before switching to a more specific objective.","title":"Description"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/feasible/#Example","page":"Feasible","text":"using ArraySynthesis; using ArraySynthesis: °, dB; using HiGHS\n\narray = uniform_linear_array(32, d = 0.5)\np = pattern(beam(0°),\n            sidelobes(region(-90°..(-5°), 1°), -25dB),\n            sidelobes(region(5°..90°,     1°), -25dB))\n\nresult = synthesize(array, p, Feasible(), ComplexWeights(), LP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formulations/socp/#SOCP","page":"SOCP","text":"Second-order cone programming formulation with exact modulus constraint.","title":"SOCP"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formulations/socp/#Syntax","page":"SOCP","text":"SOCP()","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formulations/socp/#Description","page":"SOCP","text":"Imposes AF(hatr_p) leq t_p exactly as a second-order cone constraint:\n\n(AF_re(hatr_p) AF_im(hatr_p))_2 leq t_p\n\nNo polygon approximation; the modulus bound is always exact.\n\nCompatible with linear and quadratic objectives. Typically slower than LP/QP on large models but more accurate for complex AF.\n\nAlso required when using robust, because robust bounds use one shared norm cone for the excitation vector.\n\nSuggested solvers: Clarabel.Optimizer, Mosek.Optimizer","title":"Description"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formulations/socp/#Example","page":"SOCP","text":"using ArraySynthesis; using ArraySynthesis: °, dB; using Clarabel\n\narray = uniform_linear_array(32, d = 0.5)\np = pattern(beam(0°),\n            sidelobes(region(-90°..(-5°), 1°), -20dB),\n            sidelobes(region(5°..90°,     1°), -20dB),\n            null(-40°, level = -55dB))\n\nresult = synthesize(array, p, MinPower(), ComplexWeights(), SOCP(), Clarabel.Optimizer)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/nonuniform_sll_steps/#Two-level-sidelobe-mask","page":"Two-level sidelobe mask","text":"This example follows the Tseng-Griffiths style iterative floor synthesis. The sidelobe envelope is not uniform: near sidelobes are forced lower than far sidelobes.\n\nusing ArraySynthesis\nusing ArraySynthesis: °, dB\nusing GLMakie\nusing HiGHS\n\narray = uniform_linear_array(21, d = 0.5)\ncoef = ComplexWeights()\n\nEach interval gets its own fixed level. Splitting the mask into regions makes the piecewise specification explicit.\n\nsll_far_left = region(ClosedInterval(-90°, -55.5°), 0.5°)\nsll_near_left = region(ClosedInterval(-55°, -10°), 0.5°)\nsll_near_right = region(ClosedInterval(10°, 55°), 0.5°)\nsll_far_right = region(ClosedInterval(55.5°, 90°), 0.5°)\n\np = pattern(\n    sidelobes(sll_far_left, -30dB),\n    sidelobes(sll_near_left, -40dB),\n    sidelobes(sll_near_right, -40dB),\n    sidelobes(sll_far_right, -30dB),\n)\n\nIterativeFloorSynthesis repeatedly corrects the active sidelobe peaks until the envelope is reached.\n\nobj = IterativeFloorSynthesis(ThetaDirection(0°); max_iter = 8, tol = 5e-3)\nresult = synthesize(array, p, obj, coef, QP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)\n\ntheta_vals = -π/2:0.001:π/2\naf_vals = [abs(array_factor(array, coef, result.weights, [ThetaDirection(θ)])[1]) for θ in theta_vals]\naf_db = 20 .* log10.(max.(af_vals, 1e-12))\n\nfig = Figure()\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"θ (deg)\", ylabel = \"|AF| (dB)\", title = \"Two-level SLL\")\nlines!(ax, theta_vals ./ °, af_db, linewidth = 2)\nlines!(ax, [-90, -55], [-30, -30], linestyle = :dash, color = :black)\nlines!(ax, [-55, -10], [-40, -40], linestyle = :dash, color = :black)\nlines!(ax, [10, 55], [-40, -40], linestyle = :dash, color = :black)\nlines!(ax, [55, 90], [-30, -30], linestyle = :dash, color = :black)\nylims!(ax, -80, 1)\nxlims!(ax, -90, 90)\nfig\n\nThe documentation includes a precomputed result image, so this example is shown without being executed by Documenter.\n\n(Image: Two-level sidelobe mask)\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Two-level sidelobe mask"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/robustness/#Robustness","page":"Robust synthesis","text":"Robustness lets the model reserve margin against small pointing, channel, and position errors. It is enabled with the robustness keyword in synthesize:\n\nrob = robust(\n    pointing_accuracy = 0.1°,\n    phase_tolerance = 0.5°,\n    amplitude_tolerance = 0.05dB,\n    position_tolerance = (xy = 0.0005, z = 0.0),\n)\n\nresult = synthesize(array, p, MinPower(), ComplexWeights(), SOCP(), Clarabel.Optimizer;\n                    robustness = rob)\n\nThe nominal bound\n\nAF(p) leq U(p)\n\nis replaced by\n\nAF(p) + delta(p)w_2 leq U(p)\n\nInternally this is built as one shared cone for w_2 and an affine correction delta(p)t in each affected bound. This keeps the implementation small and avoids adding one cone per sampled point.","title":"Robustness"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/robustness/#What-Is-Robustified","page":"Robust synthesis","text":"Robustness currently applies to:\n\nsidelobes\nnull\nshaped_beam\nMinSLL\n\nBeam equalities are kept nominal and act as normalization constraints. The current implementation is wired for direct single-shot solves. Iterative strategies could also pass the same robust bounds to their inner problems, but that path is not connected yet.","title":"What Is Robustified"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/robustness/#Tolerances","page":"Robust synthesis","text":"robust accepts physical tolerances:\n\npointing_accuracy: angular error, in radians for ThetaDirection; for UVDirection it is interpreted as an offset in (u, v) coordinates.\nphase_tolerance: per-channel phase error, in radians.\namplitude_tolerance: per-channel amplitude error. Values such as 0.05dB are converted to a linear relative tolerance.\nposition_tolerance: position error in wavelengths. A scalar means isotropic 3D error; (xy = ..., z = ...) separates in-plane and out-of-plane errors.\n\nThe default combination is combine = :quadrature; use combine = :sum for a more conservative direct sum.","title":"Tolerances"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/robustness/#Formulation","page":"Robust synthesis","text":"This implementation is SOCP-only because the correction uses w_2. Calling synthesize(...; robustness = rob) with LP() or QP() raises an error.\n\nDeep nulls and tight sidelobe masks can become infeasible when the uncertainty margin is larger than the available bound.","title":"Formulation"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/planar_shaped_beam/#Planar-shaped-beam","page":"Planar shaped beam","text":"This example moves from angular intervals to regions in the visible u,v plane. The main beam is a diamond-shaped region, and a separate circular region is forced to a deep null.\n\nusing ArraySynthesis\nusing ArraySynthesis: °, dB\nusing GLMakie\nusing HiGHS\n\narray_full = planar_array(16, 16, dx = 0.5, dy = 0.5)\narray = SymmetricArray(symmetrize(array_full.positions)...)\n\nuv(θ, ϕ) converts an angular direction into direction cosines. Shapes are then sampled into Regions.\n\nfocus = uv(14°, 12°)\nbeam_shape = rhombus((focus.u, focus.v), 16°)\nbeam_region = region(beam_shape, step = 2°)\n\nnull_dir = uv(-55°, -55°)\nnull_shape = ArraySynthesis.Circle(0.3, (null_dir.u, null_dir.v))\nnull_region = region(null_shape, step = 2°)\n\nThe sidelobe region is the visible square grid excluding beam and null guards.\n\nsl_region = visible_region(beam_shape, null_shape; step = 4°, bandpass = 0.12)\n\np = pattern(\n    shaped_beam(beam_region, 1.0, ripple = -1dB),\n    sidelobes(null_region, -50dB),\n)\nobj = MinSLL([sl_region])\n\nCompare a real-AF conjugate-symmetric solution with a fixed progressive phase solution centered on the desired beam.\n\nresult1 = synthesize(array, p, obj, ConjugateSymmetricWeights(), LP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)\nresult2 = synthesize(array, p, obj, ProgressivePhaseAmplitude(focus), LP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)\n\nU = V = collect(-1.0:0.02:1.0)\ndirs = [UVDirection(u, v) for u in U, v in V]\n\naf1 = array_factor(array, ConjugateSymmetricWeights(), result1.weights, dirs)\naf_vals1 = reshape(20 .* log10.(abs.(getindex.(af1, 1)) .+ 1e-12), length(U), length(V))\n\naf2 = array_factor(array, ProgressivePhaseAmplitude(focus), result2.weights, dirs)\naf_vals2 = reshape(20 .* log10.(abs.(getindex.(af2, 1)) .+ 1e-12), length(U), length(V))\n\nfig = Figure()\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"u\", ylabel = \"v\", title = \"Conjugate symmetric\")\nimage!(ax1, (-1, 1), (-1, 1), af_vals1, colorrange = (-40, 0), colormap = :viridis)\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], xlabel = \"u\", ylabel = \"v\", title = \"Progressive phase\")\nimage!(ax2, (-1, 1), (-1, 1), af_vals2, colorrange = (-40, 0), colormap = :viridis)\nColorbar(fig[1, 3], colorrange = (-40, 0), colormap = :viridis)\nfig\n\nThe documentation includes a precomputed result image, so this example is shown without being executed by Documenter.\n\n(Image: Planar shaped beam)\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Planar shaped beam"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formulations/#Formulations","page":"Formulations","text":"The AbstractFormulation controls how the modulus constraint AF(hatr_p) leq t_p is enforced and which solver types are compatible. Pass it as the fifth argument to synthesize.\n\nUse this page to choose the optimization formulation. For exact constructor signatures, see the API Reference.","title":"Formulations"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formulations/#Choosing-a-Formulation","page":"Formulations","text":"Formulation Modulus constraint Objective type Typical solver\nLP Linear Linear HiGHS\nQP Linear Linear or quadratic HiGHS\nSOCP Exact second-order cone Linear or quadratic Clarabel\n\nUse LP as the default for linear objectives, especially when the array factor is real. Use QP when the objective has a quadratic term, such as MinPower, MinIntegratedPower, or MinFieldError. Use SOCP when exact complex magnitude constraints or robust synthesis margins are important.\n\nFor a real AF (ConjugateSymmetricWeights / ProgressivePhaseAmplitude + SymmetricArray), the modulus constraint is -t leq AF leq t in all three formulations: exact and linear.","title":"Choosing a Formulation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formulations/#Formulation-Notes","page":"Formulations","text":"LP is the default for linear objectives. For complex array factors, magnitude constraints are approximated with a polygon controlled by polygon_faces.\n\nQP uses the same polygonal magnitude constraints as LP, but allows quadratic objectives such as MinPower, MinIntegratedPower, and MinFieldError.\n\nSOCP represents complex magnitude bounds exactly with second-order cones. Use it for exact complex masks and robust synthesis.","title":"Formulation Notes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/maxaf/#MaxAF","page":"MaxAF","text":"Maximize the real array-factor response at one or more directions.","title":"MaxAF"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/maxaf/#Syntax","page":"MaxAF","text":"MaxAF()\nMaxAF(direction)\nMaxAF(directions)","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/maxaf/#Arguments","page":"MaxAF","text":"Argument Description\ndirection A single direction (ThetaDirection, UVDirection, angle in radians, or (u,v) tuple).\ndirections A vector of directions.\n\nIf no argument is given, the beam directions defined in the Pattern are used.","title":"Arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/maxaf/#Description","page":"MaxAF","text":"Maximizes sum mathrmRe(AF(hatr_k)) over the specified directions, subject to the pattern constraints. Compatible with all formulations.","title":"Description"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/maxaf/#Example","page":"MaxAF","text":"using ArraySynthesis; using ArraySynthesis: °, dB; using HiGHS\n\narray = uniform_linear_array(20, d = 0.5)\np = pattern(beam(-13°),\n            sidelobes(region(-90°..(-4°), 1°), -40dB),\n            sidelobes(region(15°..90°,    1°), -40dB))\n\n# Direction taken from the beam in the pattern\nresult = synthesize(array, p, MaxAF(), ComplexWeights(), LP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)\n\n# Explicit direction\nresult = synthesize(array, p, MaxAF(-13°), ComplexWeights(), LP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minfielderror/#MinFieldError","page":"MinFieldError","text":"Minimize the L2 error between the array factor and a complex reference pattern.","title":"MinFieldError"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minfielderror/#Syntax","page":"MinFieldError","text":"MinFieldError(region, reference)","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minfielderror/#Arguments","page":"MinFieldError","text":"Argument Description\nregion A Region defining the evaluation points.\nreference Target complex values. A vector of the same length as region.points, a scalar, or a real number.","title":"Arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minfielderror/#Description","page":"MinFieldError","text":"Minimizes sum_p AF(hatr_p) - f_p^2 over the points in region, where f_p are the complex reference values. The objective is quadratic; requires QP or SOCP.\n\nThe reference phase is fixed. Use shaped_beam when the specification is an amplitude mask with ripple rather than a complex reference.","title":"Description"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minfielderror/#Example","page":"MinFieldError","text":"using ArraySynthesis; using ArraySynthesis: °, dB; using HiGHS\n\narray  = uniform_linear_array(28, d = 0.5)\nbeam_r = region(-20°..20°, 1°)\ntarget = [cos(π * p.θ / 20°) for p in beam_r.points]   # real-valued cosine target\np = pattern(sidelobes(region(-90°..(-25°), 1°), -25dB),\n            sidelobes(region(25°..90°,     1°), -25dB))\n\nresult = synthesize(array, p, MinFieldError(beam_r, target), ComplexWeights(), QP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minintegratedpower/#MinIntegratedPower","page":"MinIntegratedPower","text":"Minimize integrated power over one or more regions.","title":"MinIntegratedPower"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minintegratedpower/#Syntax","page":"MinIntegratedPower","text":"MinIntegratedPower(region)\nMinIntegratedPower(regions)","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minintegratedpower/#Arguments","page":"MinIntegratedPower","text":"Argument Description\nregion / regions A Region or a vector of Region objects over which power is integrated.","title":"Arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minintegratedpower/#Description","page":"MinIntegratedPower","text":"Minimizes sum_p AF(hatr_p)^2 over the discretized directions in the specified regions, subject to the pattern constraints. The objective is quadratic; requires QP or SOCP.","title":"Description"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minintegratedpower/#Example","page":"MinIntegratedPower","text":"using ArraySynthesis; using ArraySynthesis: °, dB; using HiGHS\n\narray = uniform_linear_array(32, d = 0.5)\ninterf_r = region(-30°..(-10°), 1°)\np = pattern(beam(0°), sidelobes(region(-90°..(-35°), 1°), -20dB),\n            sidelobes(region(5°..90°,     1°), -20dB))\n\nresult = synthesize(array, p, MinIntegratedPower(interf_r), ComplexWeights(), QP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Synthesis","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Synthesis"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Arrays","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Arrays"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Pattern","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Pattern"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Pattern-Masks","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Pattern Masks"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Region-Shapes","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Region Shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Excitations","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Excitations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Formulations","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Formulations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Objectives","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Objectives"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Iterative-Methods","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Iterative Methods"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Robustness","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Robustness"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.synthesize","page":"API Reference","text":"synthesize(array, pattern, objective, weights, formulation, solver;\n           solver_options = nothing, robustness = nothing)\n\nBuild and solve an array synthesis problem.\n\narray defines the element geometry, pattern defines the desired beams, shaped beams, nulls, and sidelobe masks, objective selects what is optimized, weights selects the excitation model, and formulation selects the JuMP constraint formulation. solver is a JuMP-compatible optimizer constructor, such as HiGHS.Optimizer or Clarabel.Optimizer.\n\nFor direct objectives, the result is a SynthesisResult. Iterative objectives return IterativeSynthesisResult, and multi-pattern iterative synthesis returns MultiPatternResult.\n\nUse solver_options to pass optimizer attributes. Use robustness = robust(...) to tighten supported constraints against tolerance margins; this requires SOCP.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.synthesize"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.array_factor","page":"API Reference","text":"array_factor(array, weights, w, points)\n\nEvaluate the array factor at the supplied directions using excitation vector w and the selected excitation model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.array_factor"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.SynthesisResult","page":"API Reference","text":"SynthesisResult\n\nResult returned by direct synthesis objectives.\n\nFields:\n\nweights: complex excitation coefficients.\nstatus: MathOptInterface termination status.\nobjective_value: optimizer objective value.\nmodel: solved JuMP model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.SynthesisResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.IterativeSynthesisResult","page":"API Reference","text":"IterativeSynthesisResult\n\nResult returned by iterative single-pattern synthesis methods.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.IterativeSynthesisResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.MultiPatternResult","page":"API Reference","text":"MultiPatternResult\n\nResult returned by multi-pattern synthesis methods.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.MultiPatternResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.ArrayGeometry","page":"API Reference","text":"ArrayGeometry(positions, dim)\n\nArray geometry with one column per element in the 3 x N positions matrix. Coordinates are expressed in wavelengths, and dim is the effective support dimension of the array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.ArrayGeometry"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.SymmetricArray","page":"API Reference","text":"SymmetricArray(positions, dim)\n\nArray represented by only one element from each conjugate-symmetric pair. Use materialize to expand it into the corresponding full ArrayGeometry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.SymmetricArray"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.uniform_linear_array","page":"API Reference","text":"uniform_linear_array(N; d = 0.5, axis = :x, centered = true)\n\nCreate a uniform linear array with N elements spaced by d wavelengths.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.uniform_linear_array"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.linear_array","page":"API Reference","text":"linear_array(x; axis = :x)\n\nCreate a linear array with element coordinates given by x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.linear_array"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.symmetric_linear_array","page":"API Reference","text":"symmetric_linear_array(N; d = 0.5, axis = :x)\n\nCreate a conjugate-symmetric representation of a centered uniform linear array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.symmetric_linear_array"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.planar_array","page":"API Reference","text":"planar_array(Nx, Ny; dx = 0.5, dy = 0.5, centered = true)\n\nCreate a rectangular planar array with Nx * Ny elements.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.planar_array"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.triangular_array","page":"API Reference","text":"triangular_array(Nx, Ny; d = 0.5, centered = true)\n\nCreate a planar triangular-lattice array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.triangular_array"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.hexagonal_array","page":"API Reference","text":"hexagonal_array(order; d = 0.5)\n\nCreate a centered hexagonal array with the given lattice order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.hexagonal_array"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.circular_array","page":"API Reference","text":"circular_array(radii, elements)\n\nCreate concentric circular rings, with elements[i] elements on radii[i].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.circular_array"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.symmetric_planar_array","page":"API Reference","text":"symmetric_planar_array(Nx, Ny; dx = 0.5, dy = 0.5, centered = true)\n\nCreate a conjugate-symmetric representative of a rectangular planar array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.symmetric_planar_array"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.symmetric_triangular_array","page":"API Reference","text":"symmetric_triangular_array(Nx, Ny; d = 0.5, centered = true)\n\nCreate a conjugate-symmetric representative of a triangular-lattice array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.symmetric_triangular_array"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.symmetric_hexagonal_array","page":"API Reference","text":"symmetric_hexagonal_array(order; d = 0.5)\n\nCreate a conjugate-symmetric representative of a hexagonal array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.symmetric_hexagonal_array"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.symmetric_circular_array","page":"API Reference","text":"symmetric_circular_array(radii, elements)\n\nCreate a conjugate-symmetric representative of concentric circular rings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.symmetric_circular_array"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.symmetrize","page":"API Reference","text":"symmetrize(full)\n\nKeep one representative per conjugate-symmetric pair in a full 3 x N position matrix and return (positions, dim).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.symmetrize"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.materialize","page":"API Reference","text":"materialize(array::SymmetricArray)\n\nExpand a symmetric representative array into its full physical ArrayGeometry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.materialize"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.is_origin","page":"API Reference","text":"is_origin(p)\n\nReturn whether point p is the array origin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.is_origin"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.is_representative","page":"API Reference","text":"is_representative(p)\n\nReturn whether point p is kept as the representative of a symmetric pair.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.is_representative"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.AbstractDirection","page":"API Reference","text":"Abstract supertype for all direction parameterizations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.AbstractDirection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.ThetaDirection","page":"API Reference","text":"ThetaDirection(θ)\n\nOne-dimensional angular direction. Angles are in radians; use ° for degrees.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.ThetaDirection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.UVDirection","page":"API Reference","text":"UVDirection(u, v)\n\nDirection in the visible (u, v) plane.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.UVDirection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.Direction","page":"API Reference","text":"Union alias for ThetaDirection and UVDirection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.Direction"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.Region","page":"API Reference","text":"Region(points, name)\n\nNamed collection of sampled directions used for masks, shaped beams, or integration regions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.Region"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.Pattern","page":"API Reference","text":"Pattern(beams, shaped_beams, null_directions, sidelobe_regions)\n\nContainer for all pattern constraints passed to synthesize. Prefer the flexible pattern constructor in user code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.Pattern"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.Beam","page":"API Reference","text":"Beam(direction, gain)\n\nMain-beam constraint at direction with the requested linear gain. Prefer the beam constructor in user code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.Beam"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.ShapedBeam","page":"API Reference","text":"ShapedBeam(region, target, ripple)\n\nShaped-beam mask over a sampled Region. Prefer shaped_beam in user code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.ShapedBeam"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.NullPoint","page":"API Reference","text":"NullPoint(direction, level)\n\nNull constraint at one direction. Prefer null in user code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.NullPoint"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.Nulls","page":"API Reference","text":"Nulls(points)\n\nCollection of null constraints. Prefer nulls in user code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.Nulls"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.SideLobeRegion","page":"API Reference","text":"SideLobeRegion(region, upper)\n\nSidelobe upper mask over a sampled Region. Prefer sidelobes in user code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.SideLobeRegion"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.ClosedInterval","page":"API Reference","text":"ClosedInterval(a, b)\na..b\n\nClosed interval helper used to create angular regions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.ClosedInterval"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.:..","page":"API Reference","text":"a..b\n\nCreate a ClosedInterval(a, b).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.:.."},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.direction","page":"API Reference","text":"direction(x)\n\nConvert numbers and (u = ..., v = ...) named tuples into direction objects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.direction"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.θ","page":"API Reference","text":"θ(x)\n\nConstruct a ThetaDirection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.θ"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.uv","page":"API Reference","text":"uv(θ, ϕ)\n\nConvert spherical angles to a UVDirection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.uv"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.region","page":"API Reference","text":"region(interval; npoints = 60, name = :region)\nregion(interval, step; name = :region)\n\nSample a one-dimensional angular interval into a Region of ThetaDirection points.\n\n\n\n\n\nregion(shape::RegionShape; step, name = :region)\n\nSample a two-dimensional shape into a Region of UVDirection points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.region"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.pattern","page":"API Reference","text":"pattern(items...)\n\nBuild a Pattern from any mixture of beam, shaped_beam, null, nulls, and sidelobes items.\n\nThis is the main user-facing pattern builder: items can be written in the order that is most natural for the synthesis problem, and pattern sorts them into the internal beam, shaped-beam, null, and sidelobe collections. All items must use compatible direction and numeric types.\n\nExamples\n\np = pattern(\n    beam(0°),\n    nulls([-30°, 30°]),\n    sidelobes(region(-90°..-10°, 1°), -25dB),\n    sidelobes(region(10°..90°, 1°), theta_ramp(10°, -30dB, 90°, -20dB)),\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.pattern"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.beam","page":"API Reference","text":"beam(dir; gain = 1.0)\n\nCreate a main-beam constraint at dir.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.beam"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.shaped_beam","page":"API Reference","text":"shaped_beam(region, target; ripple = 1.0dB, normalize = false)\n\nCreate a shaped-beam constraint over region.\n\ntarget may be a scalar, a vector with one value per region point, or a function evaluated at each point. When normalize is true, target values are scaled by their maximum absolute value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.shaped_beam"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.null","page":"API Reference","text":"null(dir; level = -60.0dB)\n\nCreate a null constraint at dir with the given maximum level.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.null"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.nulls","page":"API Reference","text":"nulls(dirs; level = -60.0dB)\n\nCreate null constraints for all directions in dirs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.nulls"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.sidelobes","page":"API Reference","text":"sidelobes(region, upper = -20dB)\n\nCreate an upper sidelobe mask over region.\n\nupper may be a scalar, a vector with one value per region point, or a function evaluated at each point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.sidelobes"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.outside","page":"API Reference","text":"outside(intervals...; limits = -90°..90°)\n\nReturn the complementary closed intervals inside limits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.outside"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.join_regions","page":"API Reference","text":"join_regions(x, y)\n\nConcatenate two sampled regions into a single Region.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.join_regions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.theta_ramp","page":"API Reference","text":"theta_ramp(θ1, y1, θ2, y2)\n\nReturn a linear mask function of ThetaDirection.θ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.theta_ramp"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.u_ramp","page":"API Reference","text":"u_ramp(u1, y1, u2, y2)\n\nReturn a linear mask function of UVDirection.u.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.u_ramp"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.v_ramp","page":"API Reference","text":"v_ramp(v1, y1, v2, y2)\n\nReturn a linear mask function of UVDirection.v.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.v_ramp"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.csc_values","page":"API Reference","text":"csc_values(region; offset = 0.1, step = 0.05)\n\nReturn cosecant-shaped sample values for the points in region.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.csc_values"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.RegionShape","page":"API Reference","text":"RegionShape\n\nAbstract type for two-dimensional shapes sampled in the visible (u, v) plane.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.RegionShape"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.Circle","page":"API Reference","text":"Circular region shape in the (u, v) plane.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.Circle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.Ellipse","page":"API Reference","text":"Elliptic region shape in the (u, v) plane.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.Ellipse"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.Rectangle","page":"API Reference","text":"Axis-aligned rectangular region shape in the (u, v) plane.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.Rectangle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.Polygon","page":"API Reference","text":"Polygonal region shape defined by vertex coordinates u and v.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.Polygon"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.Moonlike","page":"API Reference","text":"Moon-like shape defined as one disk with a second disk removed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.Moonlike"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.rhombus","page":"API Reference","text":"rhombus(center, d)\n\nCreate a rhombus-shaped Polygon centered at center.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.rhombus"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.triangle","page":"API Reference","text":"triangle(center; base, height)\n\nCreate a triangular Polygon centered at center.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.triangle"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.visible_region","page":"API Reference","text":"visible_region(shapes...; step = 1°, bandpass = 0.05, filtered = false)\n\nSample the complementary visible-region mask in the (u, v) plane.\n\nThis is the main helper for planar and conformal-array specifications where the desired beam or protected zones are easier to draw as shapes. Each supplied shape is excluded from the sampled visible plane, so the returned Region contains the directions outside all supplied shapes. Use it to define sidelobe or background regions complementary to shaped beams, null-protection zones, or other user-defined footprints.\n\nbandpass expands the excluded zone around each shape, creating a guard band between the kept visible region and the removed shapes. When filtered is true, only physically visible points satisfying u^2 + v^2 < 1 are returned; when false, the full square [-1, 1] x [-1, 1] grid is sampled.\n\nExample\n\nmain = Ellipse(0.25, 0.15, (0.0, 0.0))\nsl = visible_region(main; step = 0.02, bandpass = 0.05, filtered = true)\np = pattern(shaped_beam(region(main; step = 0.02), 1.0),\n            sidelobes(sl, -25dB))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.visible_region"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.AbstractExcitation","page":"API Reference","text":"Abstract supertype for excitation models.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.AbstractExcitation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.ComplexWeights","page":"API Reference","text":"ComplexWeights()\n\nUse independent complex excitation weights for each array element.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.ComplexWeights"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.RealWeights","page":"API Reference","text":"RealWeights()\n\nUse real-valued excitation weights for each array element.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.RealWeights"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.ConjugateSymmetricWeights","page":"API Reference","text":"ConjugateSymmetricWeights()\n\nUse conjugate-symmetric complex weights on a SymmetricArray.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.ConjugateSymmetricWeights"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.ProgressivePhaseAmplitude","page":"API Reference","text":"ProgressivePhaseAmplitude(β)\nProgressivePhaseAmplitude()\n\nUse real amplitudes with a progressive phase reference β.\n\nWhen β is omitted, the reference direction is inferred from the first beam or shaped-beam region in the pattern.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.ProgressivePhaseAmplitude"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.AbstractFormulation","page":"API Reference","text":"Abstract supertype for optimization formulations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.AbstractFormulation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.LP","page":"API Reference","text":"LP(; polygon_faces = 8)\n\nLinear-programming formulation. Complex magnitude bounds are approximated by a regular polygon with polygon_faces sides.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.LP"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.QP","page":"API Reference","text":"QP(; polygon_faces = 8)\n\nQuadratic-programming formulation with polygonal magnitude bounds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.QP"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.SOCP","page":"API Reference","text":"SOCP()\n\nSecond-order cone formulation using exact conic magnitude bounds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.SOCP"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.MILP","page":"API Reference","text":"MILP(; big_m = 10.0, polygon_faces = 8)\n\nMixed-integer formulation for sparse active-element constraints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.MILP"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.AbstractObjective","page":"API Reference","text":"Abstract supertype for all synthesis objectives.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.AbstractObjective"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.DirectObjective","page":"API Reference","text":"Abstract supertype for objectives solved with a single optimization model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.DirectObjective"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.Feasible","page":"API Reference","text":"Feasible()\n\nFind any excitation satisfying the pattern constraints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.Feasible"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.MinSLL","page":"API Reference","text":"MinSLL(region; lower_bound = 0.0, upper_bound = -20.0dB)\nMinSLL(regions; lower_bound = 0.0, upper_bound = -20.0dB)\n\nMinimize the sidelobe level over one or more regions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.MinSLL"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.MaxAF","page":"API Reference","text":"MaxAF([dirs])\n\nMaximize the real array-factor response in the supplied direction or directions. If no direction is provided, beam directions from the pattern are used.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.MaxAF"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.MinL1","page":"API Reference","text":"MinL1(; sum_limit = nothing)\n\nMinimize a convex L1-type bound on the excitation variables.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.MinL1"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.MinWeightedL1","page":"API Reference","text":"MinWeightedL1(alpha; sum_limit = nothing)\n\nMinimize a weighted convex L1-type bound with one weight per excitation variable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.MinWeightedL1"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.MinPower","page":"API Reference","text":"MinPower()\n\nMinimize total excitation power.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.MinPower"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.MinIntegratedPower","page":"API Reference","text":"MinIntegratedPower(region)\nMinIntegratedPower(regions)\n\nMinimize integrated array-factor power over one or more sampled regions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.MinIntegratedPower"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.MinFieldError","page":"API Reference","text":"MinFieldError(region, reference)\n\nMinimize squared array-factor error against a scalar or vector reference over region.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.MinFieldError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.SynthesisMethod","page":"API Reference","text":"Abstract supertype for objectives implemented as iterative synthesis loops.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.SynthesisMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.IterativeFloorSynthesis","page":"API Reference","text":"IterativeFloorSynthesis(dir; sll = -30.0dB, max_iter = 15, tol = 1e-4,\n                        stable_iterations = 3)\n\nOrchard-Elliott-Stern style iterative sidelobe-floor synthesis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.IterativeFloorSynthesis"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.IterativeReweightedL1","page":"API Reference","text":"IterativeReweightedL1(; max_iter = 15, d0 = 1e-3, stable_iterations = 3)\n\nIterative reweighted-L1 synthesis method for sparse excitations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.IterativeReweightedL1"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.MultiPatternReweightedL1","page":"API Reference","text":"MultiPatternReweightedL1(; max_iter = 15, d0 = 1e-3, stable_iterations = 3)\n\nIterative reweighted-L1 synthesis for multiple patterns sharing the same active element support.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.MultiPatternReweightedL1"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.Tolerances","page":"API Reference","text":"Tolerances\n\nTolerance data created by robust and consumed by synthesize.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.Tolerances"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ArraySynthesis.robust","page":"API Reference","text":"robust(; pointing_accuracy = 0.0,\n         phase_tolerance = 0.0,\n         amplitude_tolerance = 0.0,\n         position_tolerance = 0.0,\n         combine = :quadrature)\n\nCreate tolerance data for robust synthesis constraints.\n\nThe returned Tolerances object can be passed to synthesize through the robustness keyword. Robustness tightens supported pattern bounds by a norm margin that accounts for pointing, phase, amplitude, and position uncertainty. combine controls how contributions are accumulated and must be either :quadrature or :sum.\n\nRobust synthesis currently requires the SOCP formulation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ArraySynthesis.robust"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/narrow_beam_ramp_sll/#Narrow-beam-with-a-ramp-sidelobe-mask","page":"Ramp sidelobe mask","text":"This example uses MaxAF with explicit sidelobe constraints. The objective maximizes the response in the look direction, while the mask controls what is allowed elsewhere.\n\nusing ArraySynthesis\nusing ArraySynthesis: °, dB\nusing GLMakie\nusing HiGHS\n\narray = uniform_linear_array(20, d = 0.5)\ncoef = ComplexWeights()\n\nThe main beam is a single look direction. The middle sidelobe region uses a functional mask, so the allowed level changes with angle.\n\nsl_region1 = region(ClosedInterval(-90°, -24°), 1°)\nsl_region2 = region(ClosedInterval(-4°, 15°), 1°)\nsl_region3 = region(ClosedInterval(15°, 90°), 1°)\n\np = pattern(\n    beam(-13°),\n    sidelobes(sl_region1, -40dB),\n    sidelobes(sl_region2, theta_ramp(-4°, -20dB, 15°, -25dB)),\n    sidelobes(sl_region3, -40dB),\n)\n\nWith no explicit direction, MaxAF() uses the beam direction from p.\n\nresult = synthesize(array, p, MaxAF(), coef, LP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)\n\ntheta_vals = -π/2:0.001:π/2\naf_vals = [abs(array_factor(array, coef, result.weights, [ThetaDirection(θ)])[1]) for θ in theta_vals]\naf_db = 20 .* log10.(max.(af_vals, 1e-12))\n\nfig = Figure()\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"θ (deg)\", ylabel = \"|AF| (dB)\")\nlines!(ax, theta_vals ./ °, af_db, linewidth = 2)\nylims!(ax, -60, 10)\nxlims!(ax, -90, 90)\nfig\n\nThe documentation includes a precomputed result image, so this example is shown without being executed by Documenter.\n\n(Image: Narrow beam with ramp sidelobe mask)\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Narrow beam with a ramp sidelobe mask"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Mathematical-Background","page":"Theory","text":"","title":"Mathematical Background"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Array-Factor","page":"Theory","text":"For an N-element array with positions mathbfp_n (normalized to wavelengths) and complex excitations w_n, the array factor in direction hatr is:\n\nAF(hatr) = sum_n=1^N w_n  e^j 2pi hatr cdot mathbfp_n\n\nDiscretizing over P directions and stacking into a matrix:\n\nmathbfAF = Amathbfw qquad A_pn = e^j 2pi hatr_p cdot mathbfp_n\n\nThe synthesis problem is to find mathbfw such that mathbfAF satisfies a set of constraints (beam gain, sidelobe level, nulls), possibly optimizing some objective.","title":"Array Factor"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Symmetric-Arrays-and-Real-Array-Factor","page":"Theory","text":"A centrosymmetric array has positions in pairs pmmathbfp_n (plus optionally the origin). Imposing conjugate symmetric weights w_-n = w_n^*, each pair contributes:\n\nw_n e^j2pihatrcdotmathbfp_n + w_n^* e^-j2pihatrcdotmathbfp_n\n= 2textReleft(w_n  e^j2pihatrcdotmathbfp_nright)\n\nso the array factor becomes:\n\nAF(hatr) = sum_n Bigl\n  2cos(2pihatrcdotmathbfp_n)textRe(w_n)\n -2sin(2pihatrcdotmathbfp_n)textIm(w_n)\nBigr\n\nThis is always real. Advantages:\n\nOnly lceil N2 rceil complex weights are free optimization variables.\nThe modulus bound AF leq t collapses to -t leq AF leq t, exact and linear.\n\nSymmetricArray stores only the representative half of the array; ConjugateSymmetricWeights exploits this structure automatically.","title":"Symmetric Arrays and Real Array Factor"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Excitation-Types","page":"Theory","text":"The AbstractExcitation argument controls what the optimization variables are and how the array factor is assembled.\n\nType Array Variables AF\nComplexWeights ArrayGeometry w_n in mathbbC, free Complex\nRealWeights ArrayGeometry w_n in mathbbR Complex\nConjugateSymmetricWeights SymmetricArray lceil N2 rceil complex weights Real\nProgressivePhaseAmplitude any a_n in mathbbR, w_n = a_n e^jboldsymbolbetacdotmathbfp_n Complex (Real on SymmetricArray)\n\nProgressivePhaseAmplitude fixed phase toward a steering direction boldsymbolbeta, so the optimizer only handles real amplitudes a_n. The steering matrix is evaluated in shifted coordinates hatr - boldsymbolbeta, which makes the constraint at broadside exact and linear. On a SymmetricArray, the AF is again real for the same reason as above.","title":"Excitation Types"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Modulus-Constraints-per-Formulation","page":"Theory","text":"The fundamental sidelobe constraint is AF(hatr_p) leq t_p.\n\nSOCP — exact:\n\nAF(hatr_p) leq t_p\n\nLinear constraint: The unit disk is approximated by a regular F-faced polygon:\n\nmathrmReleft(e^jtheta_ell AF(hatr_p)right) leq t_p cos(piF)\nqquad theta_ell = frac2piellFquad ell = 0ldotsF-1\n\nThe approximation error is 1 - cos(piF) (~7.6% with the default F = 8); increasing polygon_faces reduces it.\n\nReal AF (from ConjugateSymmetricWeights or ProgressivePhaseAmplitude on a SymmetricArray): the imaginary part is zero by construction, so the bound is exact in all formulations:\n\n-t_p leq mathrmRe(AF(hatr_p)) leq t_p","title":"Modulus Constraints per Formulation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/excitations/conjugatesymmetric/#ConjugateSymmetricWeights","page":"ConjugateSymmetricWeights","text":"Conjugate-symmetric excitations for centrosymmetric arrays, producing a real array factor.","title":"ConjugateSymmetricWeights"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/excitations/conjugatesymmetric/#Syntax","page":"ConjugateSymmetricWeights","text":"ConjugateSymmetricWeights()","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/excitations/conjugatesymmetric/#Description","page":"ConjugateSymmetricWeights","text":"For a centrosymmetric array (positions in symmetric pairs pmmathbfp_n), enforcing w_-n = w_n^* makes the array factor always real:\n\nAF(hatr) = sum_n 2cos(2pihatrcdotmathbfp_n)mathrmRe(w_n)\n            - 2sin(2pihatrcdotmathbfp_n)mathrmIm(w_n)\n\nOnly the lceil N2 rceil representative weights (stored in SymmetricArray) are optimization variables.\n\nAdvantages over ComplexWeights:\n\nThe modulus constraint AF leq t reduces to -t leq AF leq t: exact and linear in all formulations, with no polygon approximation error.\nFewer optimization variables.\n\nRequires SymmetricArray (symmetric_linear_array, symmetric_planar_array, etc.).","title":"Description"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/excitations/conjugatesymmetric/#Example","page":"ConjugateSymmetricWeights","text":"using ArraySynthesis; using ArraySynthesis: °, dB; using HiGHS\n\nbeam_r = region(12.5°..37.5°, 1°)\nsll_r = join_regions(region(-90°..6.5°, 1°), region(43.5°..90°, 1°))\np = pattern(shaped_beam(beam_r, 1.0, ripple = -0.6dB))\narray = symmetric_linear_array(32, d = 0.5)\n\nresult = synthesize(array, p, MinSLL(sll_r), ConjugateSymmetricWeights(), LP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/flat_top_linear/#Flat-top-linear-beam","page":"Flat-top linear beam","text":"This example synthesizes a flat-top beam on a symmetric linear array. The shaped beam region fixes the main-lobe amplitude within a ripple tolerance, while MinSLL minimizes the sidelobe level outside that sector.\n\nusing ArraySynthesis\nusing ArraySynthesis: °, dB\nusing GLMakie\nusing HiGHS\n\nA symmetric array lets ConjugateSymmetricWeights produce a real array factor. This makes the LP sidelobe constraints exact and linear.\n\narray = symmetric_linear_array(32, d = 0.5)\n\nThe main beam is the flat sector. The sidelobe regions leave a guard band around it so the transition is not constrained too tightly.\n\nbeam_region = region(ClosedInterval(12.5°, 37.5°), 1°)\nsll_region1 = region(ClosedInterval(-90°, 6.5°), 1°)\nsll_region2 = region(ClosedInterval(43.5°, 90°), 1°)\nsll_region = join_regions(sll_region1, sll_region2)\n\nshaped_beam adds bounded-ripple constraints in the main region. MinSLL optimizes the peak sidelobe level over the sidelobe regions.\n\np = pattern(shaped_beam(beam_region, 1.0, ripple = -0.6dB))\nobj = MinSLL(sll_region)\n\nresult_cplx = synthesize(array, p, obj, ConjugateSymmetricWeights(), LP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)\n\nThe same geometry can also be synthesized with a fixed progressive phase reference. This is useful when the phase center is known a priori.\n\nphase = ProgressivePhaseAmplitude(25°)\nresult_prog = synthesize(array, p, obj, phase, LP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)\n\nPlot both solutions for comparison.\n\ntheta_vals = -π/2:0.001:π/2\naf_cplx = 20 .* log10.(max.([abs(array_factor(array, ConjugateSymmetricWeights(), result_cplx.weights, [ThetaDirection(θ)])[1]) for θ in theta_vals], 1e-12))\naf_prog = 20 .* log10.(max.([abs(array_factor(array, phase, result_prog.weights, [ThetaDirection(θ)])[1]) for θ in theta_vals], 1e-12))\n\nfig = Figure()\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"θ (deg)\", ylabel = \"|AF| (dB)\")\nlines!(ax, theta_vals ./ °, af_cplx, linewidth = 2, label = \"Conjugate symmetric\")\nlines!(ax, theta_vals ./ °, af_prog, linewidth = 2, label = \"Progressive phase\")\nylims!(ax, -60, 4)\naxislegend(ax, position = :lt)\nfig\n\nThe documentation includes a precomputed result image, so this example is shown without being executed by Documenter.\n\n(Image: Flat-top linear beam)\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Flat-top linear beam"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formulations/lp/#LP","page":"LP","text":"Linear programming formulation with polyhedral modulus constraint.","title":"LP"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formulations/lp/#Syntax","page":"LP","text":"LP()\nLP(polygon_faces = F)","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formulations/lp/#Arguments","page":"LP","text":"Argument Description\npolygon_faces Number of polygon faces for the modulus approximation (default 8).","title":"Arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formulations/lp/#Description","page":"LP","text":"Approximates AF(hatr_p) leq t_p by a regular F-faced inner polygon:\n\nmathrmRe(e^jtheta_ell AF(hatr_p)) leq t_p cos(piF)\nquad theta_ell = frac2piellF quad ell = 0ldotsF-1\n\nThe resulting model is a linear program solvable by any LP solver.\n\nThe approximation is conservative (inner polygon); the relative tightening is 1 - cos(piF). With the default F = 8 this is ~7.6%. When the AF is real (ConjugateSymmetricWeights or ProgressivePhaseAmplitude on a SymmetricArray), the constraint reduces to -t_p leq AF leq t_p, which is exact and linear regardless of polygon_faces.\n\nSuggested solvers: HiGHS.Optimizer, Mosek.Optimizer","title":"Description"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formulations/lp/#Example","page":"LP","text":"using ArraySynthesis; using ArraySynthesis: °, dB; using HiGHS\n\nbeam_r = region(12.5°..37.5°, 1°)\nsll_r = join_regions(region(-90°..6.5°, 1°), region(43.5°..90°, 1°))\np = pattern(shaped_beam(beam_r, 1.0, ripple = -0.6dB))\narray = symmetric_linear_array(32, d = 0.5)\n\nresult = synthesize(array, p, MinSLL(sll_r), ConjugateSymmetricWeights(), LP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minsll/#MinSLL","page":"MinSLL","text":"Minimize the peak sidelobe level over one or more regions.","title":"MinSLL"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minsll/#Syntax","page":"MinSLL","text":"MinSLL(region; lower_bound = 0.0, upper_bound = -20dB)\nMinSLL(regions; lower_bound = 0.0, upper_bound = -20dB)","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minsll/#Arguments","page":"MinSLL","text":"Argument Description\nregion / regions A Region or a vector of Region objects defining the sidelobe area.\nlower_bound Lower bound on the SLL auxiliary variable (default 0.0).\nupper_bound Upper bound on the SLL auxiliary variable (default -20 dB). Tighten if infeasibility occurs at the default.","title":"Arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minsll/#Description","page":"MinSLL","text":"Introduces one auxiliary variable t_i per region and minimizes sum_i t_i subject to AF(hatr_p) leq t_i for each discretized direction hatr_p in region i. Compatible with all formulations.\n\nWhen the AF is real (ConjugateSymmetricWeights or ProgressivePhaseAmplitude on a SymmetricArray), the sidelobe constraint reduces to -t_i leq AF(hatr_p) leq t_i, which is exact and linear with no polygon approximation error.","title":"Description"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minsll/#Example","page":"MinSLL","text":"Flat-top shaped beam with minimized sidelobe level:\n\nusing ArraySynthesis; using ArraySynthesis: °, dB; using HiGHS\n\nbeam_r = region(12.5°..37.5°, 1°)\nsll_r = join_regions(region(-90°..6.5°, 1°), region(43.5°..90°, 1°))\np = pattern(shaped_beam(beam_r, 1.0, ripple = -0.6dB))\narray = symmetric_linear_array(32, d = 0.5)\n\nresult = synthesize(array, p, MinSLL(sll_r), ConjugateSymmetricWeights(), LP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)\n\nMultiple independent sidelobe regions:\n\nobj = MinSLL([sll_r1, sll_r2, sll_r3])","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/sparse_multipattern/#Sparse-multipattern-array","page":"Sparse multipattern thinning","text":"This example synthesizes two different planar patterns using a shared active element support. Each pattern has its own optimized weights, but MultiPatternReweightedL1 promotes the same physical elements being active.\n\nusing ArraySynthesis\nusing ArraySynthesis: °, dB\nusing GLMakie\nusing Mosek, MosekTools\n\narray = planar_array(14, 14, dx = 0.5, dy = 0.5)\n\nPattern 1 is a broadside circular shaped beam.\n\nbeam_region1 = region(ArraySynthesis.Circle(0.2, (0.0, 0.0)), step = 2°)\nsl_region1 = visible_region(ArraySynthesis.Circle(0.4, (0.0, 0.0)); step = 4°, bandpass = 0.0, filtered = false)\np1 = pattern(\n    shaped_beam(beam_region1, 1.0, ripple = -1dB),\n    sidelobes(sl_region1, -25.85dB),\n)\n\nPattern 2 is an offset rhombus beam with an additional null.\n\nbeam_shape2 = rhombus((0.2, 0.2), 0.2)\nguard_shape2 = rhombus((0.2, 0.2), 0.4)\nbeam_region2 = region(beam_shape2, step = 2°)\nsl_region2 = visible_region(guard_shape2, ArraySynthesis.Circle(0.1, (-0.5, -0.5)); step = 4°, bandpass = 0.0, filtered = false)\nnull_region2 = region(ArraySynthesis.Circle(0.1, (-0.5, -0.5)), step = 2°)\np2 = pattern(\n    shaped_beam(beam_region2, 1.0, ripple = -1dB),\n    sidelobes(sl_region2, -24.30dB),\n    sidelobes(null_region2, -50dB),\n)\n\nobj = MultiPatternReweightedL1(max_iter = 15)\ncoef = ComplexWeights()\nresult = synthesize(array, [p1, p2], obj, coef, LP(), Mosek.Optimizer)\n\nAn element is active if it is used by any pattern.\n\nmax_activity = [maximum(abs(result.weights[k][n]) for k in eachindex(result.weights)) for n in eachindex(result.weights[1])]\nactive = max_activity .> 1e-5\n\n\nfunction normalized_db(values; floor = 1e-12)\n    mag = abs.(values)\n    mag ./= maximum(mag)\n    return 20 .* log10.(max.(mag, floor))\nend\n\nU = collect(-1.0:0.025:1.0)\nV = collect(-1.0:0.025:1.0)\ndirs = [UVDirection(u, v) for u in U, v in V]\npatterns_db = [\n    begin\n        af_db = reshape(normalized_db(array_factor(array, coef, result.weights[k], dirs)), length(U), length(V))\n        max.(af_db, -45.0)\n    end for k in eachindex(result.weights)\n]\n\naf_pattern1 = patterns_db[1]\naf_pattern2 = patterns_db[2]\nfig = Figure()\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"x/λ\", ylabel = \"y/λ\", title = \"Active: $(sum(active)) / $(length(active))\")\nscatter!(ax1, array.positions[1, .!active], array.positions[2, .!active], color = :red, marker = :x, markersize = 6)\nscatter!(ax1, array.positions[1, active], array.positions[2, active], color = :green, markersize = 8)\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], xlabel = \"u\", ylabel = \"v\", title = \"Pattern 1 AF\", aspect = DataAspect())\nimage!(ax2, (-1, 1), (-1, 1), af_pattern1, colorrange = (-40, 0), colormap = :viridis)\nax3 = Axis(fig[1, 3], xlabel = \"u\", ylabel = \"v\", title = \"Pattern 2 AF\", aspect = DataAspect())\nimage!(ax3, (-1, 1), (-1, 1), af_pattern2, colorrange = (-40, 0), colormap = :viridis)\nfig\n\nThe documentation includes a precomputed result image, so this example is shown without being executed by Documenter.\n\n(Image: Sparse multipattern array)\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Sparse multipattern array"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/iterativefloor/#IterativeFloorSynthesis","page":"IterativeFloorSynthesis","text":"Iterative QP for narrow-beam low-sidelobe synthesis.","title":"IterativeFloorSynthesis"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/iterativefloor/#Syntax","page":"IterativeFloorSynthesis","text":"IterativeFloorSynthesis(direction; sll = -30dB, max_iter = 15, tol = 1e-4, stable_iterations = 3)","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/iterativefloor/#Arguments","page":"IterativeFloorSynthesis","text":"Argument Description\ndirection Look direction for the main beam.\nsll Target SLL in linear scale (default -30dB).\nmax_iter Maximum number of iterations (default 15).\ntol Convergence tolerance (default 1e-4).\nstable_iterations Stop early if the active sidelobe set does not change for this many consecutive iterations (default 3).","title":"Arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/iterativefloor/#Description","page":"IterativeFloorSynthesis","text":"Implements a Tseng–Griffiths style iterative peak correction algorithm for prescribed sidelobe envelopes. At each step, the highest sidelobe peak in the current solution is identified and a linear floor constraint is added to the model. The process repeats until the SLL target is reached or no further improvement occurs.\n\nThe sidelobe floor constraints grow with each iteration, increasing model size. Requires QP.","title":"Description"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/iterativefloor/#References","page":"IterativeFloorSynthesis","text":"This strategy follows the linearly constrained least-squares peak-correction method in:\n\nC.-Y. Tseng and L. J. Griffiths, \"A Simple Algorithm to Achieve Desired Patterns for Arbitrary Arrays,\" IEEE Transactions on Signal Processing, vol. 40, no. 11, pp. 2737-2746, Nov. 1992.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/iterativefloor/#Example","page":"IterativeFloorSynthesis","text":"using ArraySynthesis; using ArraySynthesis: °, dB; using HiGHS\n\narray = uniform_linear_array(20, d = 0.5)\np = pattern(sidelobes(region(-90°..34°, 0.5°), -40dB),\n            sidelobes(region(74°..90°,  0.5°), -40dB))\nobj = IterativeFloorSynthesis(ThetaDirection(50°); sll = -40dB, max_iter = 8)\n\nresult = synthesize(array, p, obj, ComplexWeights(), QP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/multipatternreweightedl1/#MultiPatternReweightedL1","page":"MultiPatternReweightedL1","text":"Sparse array synthesis for multiple patterns sharing the same active element support.","title":"MultiPatternReweightedL1"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/multipatternreweightedl1/#Syntax","page":"MultiPatternReweightedL1","text":"MultiPatternReweightedL1(; max_iter = 15, d0 = 1e-3, stable_iterations = 3)","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/multipatternreweightedl1/#Arguments","page":"MultiPatternReweightedL1","text":"Argument Description\nmax_iter Maximum number of iterations (default 15).\nd0 Initial regularization (default 1e-3).\nstable_iterations Stop early if the active set is stable (default 3).","title":"Arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/multipatternreweightedl1/#Description","page":"MultiPatternReweightedL1","text":"Extension of IterativeReweightedL1 to reconfigurable arrays that must satisfy K independent patterns with a single physical aperture (same set of active elements). A shared L1 auxiliary variable couples all patterns in the sparsity objective; each pattern has independent weight variables optimized separately.\n\nPass a vector of Pattern objects as the second argument to synthesize. The result is a MultiPatternResult with one weight vector per pattern.","title":"Description"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/multipatternreweightedl1/#References","page":"MultiPatternReweightedL1","text":"This strategy follows the multiple-pattern iterative LP formulation in:\n\nY. Liu, P. You, C. Zhu, X. Tan, and Q. H. Liu, \"Synthesis of Sparse or Thinned Linear and Planar Arrays Generating Reconfigurable Multiple Real Patterns by Iterative Linear Programming,\" Progress In Electromagnetics Research, vol. 155, pp. 27-38, 2016.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/multipatternreweightedl1/#Example","page":"MultiPatternReweightedL1","text":"Two-pattern reconfigurable planar array — broadside circular beam and offset rhombus beam:\n\nusing ArraySynthesis; using ArraySynthesis: °, dB; using HiGHS\n\narray = planar_array(14, 14, dx = 0.5, dy = 0.5)\n\np1 = pattern(\n    shaped_beam(region(Circle(0.2, (0.0, 0.0)), step = 2°), 1.0, ripple = -1dB),\n    sidelobes(visible_region(Circle(0.4, (0.0, 0.0)); step = 4°, bandpass = 0.0), -25dB))\n\np2 = pattern(\n    shaped_beam(region(rhombus((0.2, 0.2), 0.2), step = 2°), 1.0, ripple = -1dB),\n    sidelobes(visible_region(rhombus((0.2, 0.2), 0.4); step = 4°, bandpass = 0.0), -24dB))\n\nobj = MultiPatternReweightedL1(max_iter = 15)\nresult = synthesize(array, [p1, p2], obj, ComplexWeights(), LP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)\n\n# Active elements: used in any of the K patterns\nmax_activity = [maximum(abs(result.weights[k][n]) for k in eachindex(result.weights))\n                for n in eachindex(result.weights[1])]\nactive = max_activity .> 1e-5","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minweightedl1/#MinWeightedL1","page":"MinWeightedL1","text":"Minimize a weighted convex L1-type bound on the optimization variables.","title":"MinWeightedL1"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minweightedl1/#Syntax","page":"MinWeightedL1","text":"MinWeightedL1(alpha; sum_limit = nothing)","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minweightedl1/#Arguments","page":"MinWeightedL1","text":"Argument Description\nalpha Weight for each optimization variable. Its length must match the number of variables.\nsum_limit Optional upper bound on the sum of the L1 auxiliary variables.","title":"Arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minweightedl1/#Description","page":"MinWeightedL1","text":"MinWeightedL1 is the weighted version of MinL1. It introduces the same auxiliary bounds and minimizes sum(alpha[n] * t[n]), so elements or variables can be penalized more heavily than others.\n\nWhen sum_limit is provided, the model also imposes an upper bound on sum(t).\n\nThis is a direct convex objective, not the sparse/thinned-array strategy. Sparse thinning is implemented by IterativeReweightedL1 and MultiPatternReweightedL1, which repeatedly update these weights.","title":"Description"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/objectives/minweightedl1/#Example","page":"MinWeightedL1","text":"using ArraySynthesis; using ArraySynthesis: °, dB; using HiGHS\n\narray = uniform_linear_array(16, d = 0.5)\np = pattern(beam(0°),\n            sidelobes(region(-90°..(-5°), 1°), -25dB),\n            sidelobes(region(5°..90°, 1°), -25dB))\nalpha = ones(2 * 16)\n\nresult = synthesize(array, p, MinWeightedL1(alpha), ComplexWeights(), LP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/excitations/realweights/#RealWeights","page":"RealWeights","text":"Real-valued excitations, one per element.","title":"RealWeights"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/excitations/realweights/#Syntax","page":"RealWeights","text":"RealWeights()","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/excitations/realweights/#Description","page":"RealWeights","text":"Each element has a real weight w_n in mathbbR. The array factor is still complex in general (because the steering matrix A is complex), but the number of optimization variables is halved compared to ComplexWeights.\n\nAppropriate when the feeding network physically constrains all excitations to be real-valued (e.g. uniform-phase corporate feed, Taylor distribution starting point).\n\nNot to be confused with ConjugateSymmetricWeights, which enforces a symmetry condition that makes the array factor real. RealWeights only constrains the weights, not the AF.\n\nUse with ArrayGeometry.\nCompatible with all formulations.","title":"Description"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/patterns/#Pattern-and-Regions","page":"Pattern and regions","text":"pattern is the main user-facing specification layer in ArraySynthesis. It collects beams, shaped beams, nulls, and sidelobe masks in whatever order is most natural for the problem:\n\np = pattern(\n    beam(0°),\n    nulls([-30°, 30°]),\n    sidelobes(region(-90°..-10°, 1°), -25dB),\n    sidelobes(region(10°..90°, 1°), theta_ramp(10°, -30dB, 90°, -20dB)),\n)\n\nThe individual items are sorted internally into a Pattern, which is then passed to synthesize.","title":"Pattern and Regions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/patterns/#Direction-Systems","page":"Pattern and regions","text":"One-dimensional cuts use ThetaDirection:\n\nθ0 = θ(15°)\nθ0 = ThetaDirection(15°)\n\nPlanar and conformal-array masks usually use direction cosines:\n\np = UVDirection(0.2, -0.1)\np = direction((u = 0.2, v = -0.1))\n\nThe helper uv(θ, ϕ) converts spherical angles into a UVDirection.","title":"Direction Systems"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/patterns/#One-Dimensional-Regions","page":"Pattern and regions","text":"Use closed intervals and region for angular cuts:\n\nmain = region(-10°..10°, 0.5°)\nsll_left = region(-90°..-15°, 1°)\nsll_right = region(15°..90°, 1°)\n\noutside builds complementary intervals, which is convenient for linear arrays:\n\nmain_lobe = -12°..12°\nsll = join_regions(region.(outside(main_lobe), 1°)...)","title":"One-Dimensional Regions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/patterns/#Pattern-Items","page":"Pattern and regions","text":"Use beam for exact gain constraints:\n\nbeam(0°)\nbeam((u = 0.1, v = 0.0), gain = 1.0)\n\nUse shaped_beam when the main region has a mask rather than a single direction:\n\nflat = shaped_beam(region(-10°..10°, 1°), 1.0, ripple = -0.5dB)\ntilted = shaped_beam(region(-10°..10°, 1°), theta_ramp(-10°, 0.8, 10°, 1.0))\n\nUse sidelobes for upper masks:\n\nsidelobes(region(15°..90°, 1°), -25dB)\nsidelobes(region(15°..90°, 1°), theta_ramp(15°, -35dB, 90°, -20dB))\n\nUse null and nulls for deep constraints at isolated directions:\n\nnull(30°)\nnulls([-30°, 30°], level = -60dB)","title":"Pattern Items"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/patterns/#Planar-Complementary-Regions","page":"Pattern and regions","text":"For planar and conformal arrays, the easiest specification is often geometric: draw the beam or protected footprint in the (u, v) plane, then synthesize over the complementary visible region. That is the role of visible_region.\n\nbeam_shape = Ellipse(0.25, 0.15, (0.0, 0.0))\nbeam_region = region(beam_shape; step = 0.02)\nsll_region = visible_region(beam_shape; step = 0.02, bandpass = 0.05,\n                             filtered = true)\n\np = pattern(\n    shaped_beam(beam_region, 1.0, ripple = -0.5dB),\n    sidelobes(sll_region, -25dB),\n)\n\nvisible_region(shapes...) samples the (u, v) visible plane and removes all directions inside the supplied shapes. bandpass expands the removed zones to leave a guard band between the shaped/protected region and the sidelobe mask.\n\nAvailable shapes include Circle, Ellipse, Rectangle, Polygon, Moonlike, and convenience constructors such as rhombus and triangle.","title":"Planar Complementary Regions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/excitations/complexweights/#ComplexWeights","page":"ComplexWeights","text":"Fully free complex excitations, one per element.","title":"ComplexWeights"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/excitations/complexweights/#Syntax","page":"ComplexWeights","text":"ComplexWeights()","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/excitations/complexweights/#Description","page":"ComplexWeights","text":"Each element has an independent complex weight w_n in mathbbC. No structure is imposed on the weights; this is the most general excitation model.\n\nThe array factor is complex and the modulus constraint AF(hatr_p) leq t_p is approximated by a polygon (LP, QP) or imposed exactly as a second-order cone (SOCP).\n\nUse with ArrayGeometry or SymmetricArray (via materialize).\nCompatible with all formulations and all objectives.","title":"Description"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/excitations/complexweights/#Example","page":"ComplexWeights","text":"using ArraySynthesis; using ArraySynthesis: °, dB; using HiGHS\n\narray = uniform_linear_array(20, d = 0.5)\np = pattern(beam(-13°),\n            sidelobes(region(-90°..(-4°), 1°), -40dB),\n            sidelobes(region(15°..90°,    1°), -40dB))\n\nresult = synthesize(array, p, MaxAF(), ComplexWeights(), LP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"#ArraySynthesis.jl","page":"Home","text":"A Julia package for antenna array factor synthesis via convex optimization. Supports LP, QP, and SOCP formulations with multiple solvers through JuMP.\n\n(Image: Synthesized array patterns)","title":"ArraySynthesis.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"pkg> add https://github.com/uvegege/ArraySynthesis.jl","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"using ArraySynthesis\nusing ArraySynthesis: °, dB\nusing HiGHS\n\nbeam_region = region(12.5°..37.5°, 1°)\nsll_region1 = region(-90°..6.5°, 1°)\nsll_region2 = region(43.5°..90°, 1°)\n\np = pattern(shaped_beam(beam_region, 1.0, ripple = -0.6dB))\nobj = MinSLL(join_regions(sll_region1, sll_region2))\narray = symmetric_linear_array(32, d = 0.5)\n\nresult = synthesize(array, p, obj, ConjugateSymmetricWeights(), LP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Documentation-Map","page":"Home","text":"Start with User Guide for the five pieces of a synthesis problem: array, pattern, objective, excitation, and formulation.\n\nSee Pattern and Regions for the pattern specification.\n\nThe Objectives, Excitations, and Formulations pages describe the main modeling choices.","title":"Documentation Map"},{"category":"section","location":"#References","page":"Home","text":"H. Lebret and S. Boyd, \"Antenna array pattern synthesis via convex optimization,\" IEEE Trans. Signal Process., vol. 45, no. 3, pp. 526–532, Mar. 1997.\nB. Fuchs, \"Shaped beam synthesis of arbitrary arrays via linear programming,\" IEEE Antennas Wireless Propag. Lett., vol. 9, pp. 481–484, 2010.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#Implementation-Notes","page":"Implementation notes","text":"Short notes on the code.","title":"Implementation Notes"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#Common-array-factor-expressions","page":"Implementation notes","text":"array_factor_reim builds common real/imaginary array-factor expressions for the supported array and excitation types. Constraints and objectives then reuse those expressions instead of maintaining separate matrix-form code paths for every combination of array, excitation, formulation, and objective.","title":"Common array-factor expressions"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#Manual-AffExpr-construction","page":"Implementation notes","text":"polygon_bound! builds each linear combination with add_to_expression!. This avoids the intermediate allocations from generic JuMP expression arithmetic in the innermost sidelobe-point / polygon-face loop.","title":"Manual AffExpr construction"},{"category":"section","location":"implementation/#Solver-choice-for-large-problems","page":"Implementation notes","text":"HiGHS and Clarabel are fine for small examples. Large planar problems or dense sampling grids are typically much faster with Mosek.","title":"Solver choice for large problems"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/workflow/#User-Guide","page":"Overview","text":"ArraySynthesis problems are built from five pieces:\n\nresult = synthesize(array, pattern, objective, excitation, formulation, solver)\n\nEach piece answers a different question.\n\nPiece Question Common choices\narray Where are the elements? uniform_linear_array, planar_array, symmetric_linear_array\npattern What should the array factor satisfy? pattern(beam(...), sidelobes(...), nulls(...))\nobjective What should be optimized? MinSLL, MaxAF, MinPower, MinL1\nexcitation How are weights parametrized? ComplexWeights, ConjugateSymmetricWeights\nformulation How are magnitude constraints represented? LP, QP, SOCP","title":"User Guide"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/workflow/#Arrays","page":"Overview","text":"Array coordinates are expressed in wavelengths and stored as a 3 x N matrix with one column per element. Convenience constructors cover the most common layouts:\n\narray = uniform_linear_array(32, d = 0.5)\narray = planar_array(16, 16, dx = 0.5, dy = 0.5)\narray = circular_array([0.0, 1.0, 2.0], [1, 8, 16])\n\nUse SymmetricArray constructors when the geometry is centrosymmetric and the excitation model can exploit that symmetry:\n\narray = symmetric_linear_array(32, d = 0.5)\ncoef = ConjugateSymmetricWeights()\n\nThis stores only one representative of each symmetric pair and gives a real array factor for conjugate-symmetric weights.","title":"Arrays"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/workflow/#Patterns","page":"Overview","text":"The pattern is the constraint specification. It is intentionally written as a small DSL so that the problem statement stays close to the antenna specification:\n\np = pattern(\n    beam(0°),\n    nulls([-35°, 35°]),\n    sidelobes(region(-90°..-10°, 1°), -25dB),\n    sidelobes(region(10°..90°, 1°), -25dB),\n)\n\nSee Pattern and Regions for the detailed guide, including planar and conformal-array regions with visible_region.","title":"Patterns"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/workflow/#Solving","page":"Overview","text":"Linear objectives and real array factors usually work well with LP() and HiGHS. Quadratic objectives need QP(). Exact complex magnitude constraints and robust synthesis use SOCP().\n\nusing ArraySynthesis\nusing ArraySynthesis: °, dB\nusing HiGHS\n\narray = symmetric_linear_array(32, d = 0.5)\nsll = join_regions(region(-90°..6.5°, 1°), region(43.5°..90°, 1°))\np = pattern(\n    shaped_beam(region(12.5°..37.5°, 1°), 1.0, ripple = -0.6dB),\n)\n\nresult = synthesize(array, p, MinSLL(sll),\n                    ConjugateSymmetricWeights(), LP(), HiGHS.Optimizer)\n\nThe returned result contains the synthesized complex weights:\n\nw = result.weights\n\nUse array_factor to evaluate the resulting pattern on any sampled grid.","title":"Solving"}]
}
